def main():
    aux=[]
    def add(n,s,pages,note,deps=()):aux.append(dict(local_id='A'+str(n),statement_original=s,evidence=[dict(page=p,location='Original main-text context A'+str(n)) for p in pages],scope_note=note,depends_on=list(deps)))
    add(1,r'''$\mathbf I_r$ represents the $r$-by-$r$ identity matrix. Let $\mathbb O_{p,r}=\{\mathbf U\in\mathbb R^{p\times r}:\mathbf U^\top\mathbf U=\mathbf I_r\}$ and for any $\mathbf U\in\mathbb O_{p,r}$, denote $P_{\mathbf U}=\mathbf U\mathbf U^\top$.''',[7],'Original orthonormal-frame and orthogonal-projector definitions, retained as ambient notation without inventing a source natural-language Stiefel title. Used by best Tucker approximation, geometry and OHOOI. No availability claim is made.')
    add(2,r'''To this end, we adopt a rank over-parameterization scheme: we introduce a conservative guess of rank $\mathbf r:=(r_1,\ldots,r_d,r_{d+1},\ldots,r_{d+m})\ge(r_1^*,\ldots,r_d^*,r_{d+1}^*,\ldots,r_{d+m}^*)$ and solve the following tensor-on-tensor regression under the possibly over-parameterized regime:''',[3],'Standing componentwise input rank at least true rank, including the feasible-true-parameter convention for Theorem3. Theorem9 rebinds its rank-one testing distribution; Theorem10 states its own rank inequality.',['D2','D6'])
    add(3,ALGORITHM_ONE,[10],'Full original Algorithm1, including both alternatives and named retraction examples. D11 and D31 select the RGD and RGN branches, with common input/output; this full source remains intact and is not silently rewritten.',['D1','D2','D7','D8','D9','D10'])
    add(4,r'''Then the projection operator onto $\mathbf U_{k\perp}^t$, the orthogonal complement of $\mathbf U_k^t$, satisfies
\[
P_{\mathbf U_{k\perp}^t}=\mathbf I_{p_k}-P_{\mathbf U_k^t}=\mathbf I_{p_k}-P_{\overline{\mathbf U}_k^t}-P_{\check{\mathbf U}_k^t}=(\mathbf I_{p_k}-P_{\overline{\mathbf U}_k^t})(\mathbf I_{p_k}-P_{\check{\mathbf U}_k^t}).
\]
(17)''',[19],'Original orthogonal-complement convention. The barred/check decomposition is separately A5. Theorem10 applies a complement to the ESTIMATED initial subspace, not the true factor. This notation passage is not an import of the proof lemma that follows.')
    add(5,r'''for any $k\in[d+m]$, the mode-$k$ singular subspace of $\mathcal X^t$, denoted by $\mathbf U_k^t$, can be decomposed as $\mathbf U_k^t=[\overline{\mathbf U}_k^t\ \check{\mathbf U}_k^t]$ where $\overline{\mathbf U}_k^t$ is composed of the first $r_k^*$ columns of $\mathbf U_k^t$ and $\check{\mathbf U}_k^t$ is composed of the rest of the $(r_k-r_k^*)$ columns of $\mathbf U_k^t$.''',[19],'Only the source notation accompanying the orthogonal-complement identity A4. Its use in a proof does not add a dependency of every theorem on Lemma3.',['D2','D3'])
    add(6,STATEMENTS[0].split(' In addition, ',1)[1].split(' Then for all',1)[0],[10],'Theorem1’s separate RGD TRIP upper and signal-to-noise lower bounds. These are assumed in T1 and are not transferred into T2 merely because lambda is defined there. The initialization conditions promised in T5/T6/T8 are separately D24/D25.',['D5','D8','D12','D13'])
    add(7,r'''Consider the (over-parameterized) matrix trace regression model in (12) with $r\ge r^*$. Let $\mathcal H_r$ be the rank $r$ truncated SVD. Suppose $\mathscr A$ satisfies $2r$-RIP.''',[15],'Corollary1’s surrounding context for the matrix initialization conditions referenced in T7. These hypotheses are distinguished from the closeness property guaranteed for its output.',['D12','D19','D20'])
    add(8,r'''In addition, we assume $R_{2r}\le\frac1{17}$ and $\sigma_{r^*}(\mathbf X^*)\ge\frac{4(1+R_{2r+r^*}-R_{2r})}{R_{2r}(1-R_{2r})}\|(\mathscr A^*(\boldsymbol\varepsilon))_{\max(2r)}\|_{\mathrm F}$.''',[15],'Additional RGD-only Corollary1 conditions, separate from its initialization-distance inequality and RGN branch. Preserve sharper matrix constants.',['D3','D5','D8','D12'])
    add(9,r'''The classic RGD/RGN methods are designed to optimize on smooth manifolds. This corresponds to minimizing the objective function in (3) with the fixed Tucker rank constraint $\operatorname{Tucrank}(\mathcal X)=\mathbf r$ since $\mathbb M_{\mathbf r}$ is a smooth manifold. Note that $\{\mathcal X:\operatorname{Tucrank}(\mathcal X)\le\mathbf r\}$ is not a smooth manifold while such bounded rank constraint is essential to handle over-parameterization, the classic theory no longer applies. Regardless, we propose to continue using Algorithm 1 even with the bounded rank constraint.''',[9],'Remark1 explicitly distinguishes the smooth exact-rank manifold from the bounded-rank feasible set. Do not redefine the latter as smooth or quietly assume rank-deficient tangent/QR choices have been specified.',['D2','D6','D7','D11','D31'])
    add(10,r'''Throughout the paper, let $c(d)$ be a constant that depends on $d$ only, whose actual value varies from line to line; $c_1(m),c_2(d,m)$ are noted similarly. Finally, we denote $\mathscr A^*$ as the adjoint of the linear operator $\mathscr A$.''',[7,8],'Dimension-dependent constants have only the displayed mode-order dependence, not a new dependence on sample size or ambient dimensions. The adjoint is defined explicitly in D8.',['D8'])
    issues=[]
